                <pb facs="0226" n="176" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav03/pg176"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 661.939/3</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Reinsch</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-04-30T12:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 30, 1918,
                                noon</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received April 30,
                            10.17 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Referring to your telegram April 25, 5 p.m. There is no change in the embargo
                    matter. The Chinese Government feels embargo is a great detriment but will
                    maintain it for the sake of Allies while they insist. The British and French
                    Governments continue to insist on embargo, Russian Minister believing that
                    export of foodstuffs to regions east of Irkutsk not dangerous but desirable.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Reinsch</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
